1
00:00:00,884 --> 00:00:08,860
Welcome to Spain's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,519 --> 00:00:20,734
We work through 16 of the Spain regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:24,081
Let's bring Asturias into view.

4
00:00:30,169 --> 00:00:40,188
Asturias sits on Spain’s north-western coast, between Galicia and Cantabria,
with the Cantabrian mountains between the sea and the plain.

5
00:00:40,847 --> 00:00:48,347
It is the one part of Iberia the Moors never held, which is why the heir to
the Spanish throne is called the Prince of Asturias.

6
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:55,616
Remember the green coast that was never conquered, and the homeland of the
heir to the throne.

7
00:00:56,321 --> 00:00:59,862
Let's bring Cantabria into view.

8
00:01:06,807 --> 00:01:15,979
Cantabria faces the Bay of Biscay between Asturias and the Basque Country,
with its largest city, Santander, at the mouth of the river.

9
00:01:16,637 --> 00:01:27,771
The Altamira caves here hold paintings around 36,000 years old, and when they
were found in 1880 nobody believed they were prehistoric.

10
00:01:28,431 --> 00:01:35,165
Remember the green north that gets more rain than London, and the cave
paintings that rewrote the story of art.

11
00:01:35,849 --> 00:01:39,262
Let's bring País Vasco into view.

12
00:01:45,866 --> 00:01:54,864
The Basque Country sits in the north-coast corner where Spain meets France,
with Bilbao on the estuary of the Nervión.

13
00:01:55,527 --> 00:02:05,327
Basque is unrelated to any other living language on earth and was spoken here
before Indo-European reached Europe; the region calls itself Euskadi.

14
00:02:05,982 --> 00:02:14,214
Remember the language that is a cousin of nobody, and the steel port the
Guggenheim turned into a destination.

15
00:02:14,921 --> 00:02:16,755
Let's bring Galicia into view.

16
00:02:23,689 --> 00:02:33,836
Galicia fills Spain’s north-western corner above Portugal, with its coast cut
into rías, drowned river valleys.

17
00:02:34,499 --> 00:02:46,829
Santiago de Compostela stands here, the end of the pilgrim road, and Galician
was close enough to Portuguese that the two were one language until the 1300s.

18
00:02:47,509 --> 00:02:54,370
Remember the drowned valleys and the end of the pilgrimage, where Galician and
Portuguese were once the same tongue.

19
00:02:55,064 --> 00:02:56,980
Let's bring Navarra into view.

20
00:03:06,911 --> 00:03:14,794
Navarra straddles the Pyrenees, north of La Rioja and east of the Basque
Country, with Pamplona as its capital.

21
00:03:15,519 --> 00:03:27,756
It was an independent kingdom for seven centuries and France kept the northern
half until 1620; the bulls still run through Pamplona every July.

22
00:03:28,436 --> 00:03:37,608
Remember the small kingdom that straddled the mountains, half French until
1620, with the running of the bulls at its capital.

23
00:03:38,327 --> 00:03:40,579
Let's bring La Rioja into view.

24
00:03:54,592 --> 00:04:02,731
La Rioja is a small region on the upper Ebro, between Navarra and Castilla y
León, north of Zaragoza.

25
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:13,455
It is Spain’s smallest mainland region and the one whose name appears on more
bottles than any other; Spanish was first written down at the San Millán
monastery here.

26
00:04:14,113 --> 00:04:23,157
Remember the smallest region, the wine label on every table, and the monastery
where Spanish was first written down.

27
00:04:23,915 --> 00:04:26,852
Let's bring Cataluña into view.

28
00:04:44,544 --> 00:04:52,938
Cataluña occupies Spain’s north-eastern corner, facing the Mediterranean
between the Pyrenees and the Valencian border.

29
00:04:53,644 --> 00:05:07,425
Barcelona was the Frankish empire’s southern march against the Moors, which is
why the region faces the Mediterranean and France more than Madrid; Catalan is
spoken by about nine million people.

30
00:05:08,087 --> 00:05:17,468
Remember the Mediterranean corner that faces France, with nine million Catalan
speakers and a basilica building since 1882.

31
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:21,520
Let's bring Castilla y León into view.

32
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:46,319
Castilla y León fills Spain’s north-western interior, touching nine regions,
with Valladolid in the middle.

33
00:05:46,982 --> 00:05:57,048
Castilla is “land of castles”, the frontier forts against the Moors, and León
is not the lion but the Latin legio, the Seventh Legion’s camp.

34
00:05:57,712 --> 00:06:06,454
Remember the largest region, bigger than Portugal, whose arms carry a pun: the
lion is really a legion.

35
00:06:07,492 --> 00:06:09,919
Let's bring Aragón into view.

36
00:06:23,151 --> 00:06:30,233
Aragón spreads across Spain’s north-eastern interior, from the Pyrenees down
to the arid Monegros south of Zaragoza.

37
00:06:30,894 --> 00:06:42,748
Its marriage to Castilla in 1469 made Spain, and its own crown had already
taken Sicily, Sardinia and Naples.

38
00:06:43,630 --> 00:06:51,223
Remember the kingdom that married itself into Spain, with the highest peaks
outside the Sierra Nevada above it.

39
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:54,085
Let's bring Madrid into view.

40
00:07:07,924 --> 00:07:16,028
Madrid sits almost in the middle of Spain, where the country folds together,
and its region has no sea.

41
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:29,183
It was a provincial town until Philip II moved the court there in 1561, and at
650 metres it is the highest capital in Europe.

42
00:07:29,867 --> 00:07:38,133
Remember the capital chosen for being in the middle, at 650 metres the highest
in Europe, with the Prado’s Spanish painting.

43
00:07:39,344 --> 00:07:43,558
Let's bring Castilla-La Mancha into view.

44
00:07:52,787 --> 00:08:01,959
Castilla-La Mancha is the high plain south of Madrid and east of Extremadura,
between the mountains and the Mediterranean coast.

45
00:08:02,616 --> 00:08:12,043
Its name means “the dry land”, from the Arabic, and Don Quixote rode here; the
windmills he charged still stand on the ridge at Consuegra.

46
00:08:12,705 --> 00:08:18,371
Remember the dry land of Don Quixote, with his windmills still standing on the
ridge.

47
00:08:19,095 --> 00:08:21,649
Let's bring Islas Baleares into view.

48
00:08:31,801 --> 00:08:41,565
The Balearic Islands lie in the Mediterranean east of the Valencian coast:
Mallorca, Menorca and Ibiza.

49
00:08:42,231 --> 00:08:53,191
Mallorca and Menorca are simply the larger and the smaller, from the Latin
maior and minor, and the prehistoric stone towers called talayots stand on
Menorca.

50
00:08:53,844 --> 00:09:00,067
Remember the islands of the greater and the lesser, with the prehistoric stone
towers of Menorca.

51
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:04,080
Let's bring Comunidad Valenciana into view.

52
00:09:08,749 --> 00:09:17,665
The Valencian Community runs down Spain’s east coast, from the Pyrenees past
Valencia to the border with Murcia.

53
00:09:18,318 --> 00:09:28,639
Its Albufera lagoon is where paella comes from, cooked over orange wood with
rabbit and beans rather than seafood, and the region grows most of Spain’s
oranges.

54
00:09:29,298 --> 00:09:37,147
Remember the orange coast and the lagoon where paella was born, with rabbit
and beans instead of seafood.

55
00:09:37,844 --> 00:09:40,015
Let's bring Extremadura into view.

56
00:09:45,698 --> 00:09:51,793
Extremadura is the western region between Portugal and Andalucía, north of the
Sierra Morena.

57
00:09:52,449 --> 00:10:04,605
Its name means “the land beyond the Duero”, the outer edge of the reconquered
frontier, and Cortés, Pizarro and Balboa all came from this one province.

58
00:10:05,256 --> 00:10:12,501
Remember the land beyond the Duero, which sent three conquerors to the
Americas and feeds the black pigs of ibérico ham.

59
00:10:13,355 --> 00:10:15,607
Let's bring Murcia into view.

60
00:10:23,495 --> 00:10:32,156
Murcia is the small south-eastern region between the Valencian coast and
Andalucía, around the city of the same name.

61
00:10:32,813 --> 00:10:46,072
Its huerta, the irrigated orchard, is watered by canals the Moors dug eleven
centuries ago that still run on the same gradients, and the region grows
vegetables for half the continent.

62
00:10:46,722 --> 00:10:54,733
Remember the orchard of Europe, green from eleven-century Moorish canals in
one of the driest places in Spain.

63
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:58,367
Let's bring Andalucía into view.

64
00:11:12,423 --> 00:11:21,502
Andalucía fills Spain’s southern third, from the Atlantic at Huelva to the
Mediterranean at Almería, with Seville in the middle.

65
00:11:22,302 --> 00:11:32,623
Its name comes from al-Andalus, the Moorish name for all of Iberia, and the
Moors held it for nearly eight hundred years, until 1492.

66
00:11:33,289 --> 00:11:42,623
Remember al-Andalus, held for eight hundred years, with the Alhambra and
flamenco and the southernmost ski resort in Europe above Granada.

67
00:11:59,839 --> 00:12:27,919
16 regions of Spain, one for each target of the map: Asturias, Cantabria, País
Vasco, Galicia, Navarra, La Rioja, Cataluña, Castilla y León, Aragón, Madrid,
Castilla-La Mancha, Islas Baleares, Comunidad Valenciana, Extremadura, Murcia
and Andalucía.

68
00:12:28,571 --> 00:12:38,254
Remember the shape of Spain from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
